wolf3d-portable @PROJECT_VERSION@ - Windows release
=====================================

This folder contains:

  wolf3d.exe              Win32 video, input, timing, and audio host
  wolf3d.dll              Platform-neutral Wolf3D engine
@W3P_OPL_FILE_LINE@
  README.TXT             Getting started and runtime options
  DOCS/                  Build details, component notices and licenses

Official packages embed the MSVC runtime, so the Microsoft Visual C++
Redistributable is not required on the destination system. Custom builds made
with WG_STATIC_MSVC_RUNTIME=OFF require the matching Redistributable.

Game data is not included. Copy the files from an original supported game into
this folder beside wolf3d.exe, then run the executable. Supported data
extensions are WL1, WL6, SOD, SDM, SD1, SD2, and SD3.

The window aspect-corrects the original 320x200 VGA framebuffer to 4:3.
Resizing to another shape keeps the game centered at 4:3 with black bars.
Press F11 or Alt+Enter to toggle borderless desktop fullscreen. F11 is handled
only by the host and will not dismiss an in-game "Press a key" prompt.

The original SIGNON and PC-13 screens appear automatically. SIGNON artwork is
embedded in the engine as it was in the DOS executable: WL1 selects Apogee,
WL6 selects GT, and Spear selects its own screen. Use --signon apogee, gt, id,
activision, or spear to override that choice. Its memory and hardware markers
are filled at runtime using the original screen coordinates and color ramps.
Wolf3D displays the original Press a key/Working prompts; Spear holds SIGNON
for three seconds automatically.
Use --adlib or original -nosb for an AdLib-only machine. Use --pc-speaker or
original -noal for no detected sound card with PC-speaker effects, or
--no-sound for no detected sound card with all in-game sound initially off.
SIGNON and the Sound menu reflect the selected emulated hardware.
Use --opl nuked, --opl dbopl, or --opl silent to select a driver recorded in
DOCS/BUILD.TXT. Use --sample-rate HZ to change the preferred PCM rate.
Mouse and XInput joystick hardware are detected automatically. Use --mouse or
--joy to force a device present, and --nomouse or --nojoy to force it absent.
An enabled mouse is captured inside the window and released when the game
loses focus.

The launcher recursively scans this folder and ordinary subfolders for
complete game-data sets. If multiple directories contain the selected
extension, choose one explicitly with --data PATH. Select an extension with
either form:

  wolf3d.exe --game WL6
  wolf3d.exe -WL1

Names beginning with "wolf" prefer WL6 then WL1. The exact basename "spear"
prefers SOD then SDM. Either falls back to the other family if needed. Mission
packs SD1/SD2/SD3 are selected only by --game or -SD1/-SD2/-SD3. You
may instead keep game files elsewhere and pass --data "C:\path\to\game".

Optional defaults may be placed in wolf3d.ini beside the executable, one
command-line option per line. A wrapper-specific file such as
wolf3d-sdl3.ini takes priority when applicable. Real command-line arguments
override matching defaults. Use --config FILE, --no-config, or --diag for an
explicit file, a clean launch, or a hardware/data report without starting.

@W3P_OPL_KEEP_NOTE@

Original CONFIG.<EXT> and SAVEGAMn.<EXT> files are stored in the selected
game-data directory and retain that game's data extension.

Run wolf3d.exe --help (also -h or /?) from a terminal to list all game and
Win32 host options plus the OPL drivers compiled into this package.

DOCS/BUILD.TXT records the complete configured build options, compiler and
source details. See docs/BUILD-NAMING.md in the source tree for package naming.
